<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The whole network policy of the booth app, in one file.

  Cleartext HTTP is PERMITTED, because the one thing this app has to talk to is a booth laptop
  serving plain HTTP on a private LAN, at a DHCP address, with no certificate and no DNS name.
  There is nothing to get a certificate from at a trade show with no internet, and the address
  cannot be listed here because it is discovered at run time.

  WHY THIS CHANGED — 2026-08-07. This file used to permit cleartext to 127.0.0.1 and localhost
  ONLY. That reads as the safe choice and it silently broke the entire point of the app: the
  tablet could not drive the stick. `BoothBus.health()` wraps its probe in runCatching, so a
  cleartext-blocked kiosk is indistinguishable from a kiosk that is not there — the reel just
  kept playing and nothing ever said why. It appeared to work on the bench only because
  `adb reverse` makes the laptop look like loopback, which is exactly the address this file
  allowed. On the show floor the kiosk is 192.168.x.x and every probe failed.

  The narrowing happens one layer up instead, the same way the tablet app does it: BoothBus
  only ever speaks to an address that answered `GET /health` — the beacon's host and port, an
  address saved after a successful probe, or one typed on the remote — and it speaks to nothing
  else. Film downloads are unaffected: they are https R2 URLs and stay https.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
